A dense syntax reference and fetch strategy for an LLM or agent emitting tramaj templates.

=base:main-content.cmark

# tramaj for coding agents

tramaj was designed to be as easy for a model to emit correctly as for a
person to write by hand — a small builtin set, one expression grammar, and
no template/expression phase split to keep track of. This page is a dense
cheatsheet plus a fetch strategy for getting the full grammar when the
summary below isn't enough.

## Fetch the canonical source first

Don't rely on prose alone for anything you're about to emit — pull the
normative text:

- Every page on this site is also served as plain text at
  `/text/<page-name>.cmark.text` (e.g. `/text/reference-language.cmark.text`)
  — no HTML to strip.
- The specs themselves are the ultimate source of truth, on GitHub:
  [`reference.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/reference.md),
  [`node-json.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/node-json.md),
  [`v3-symbols.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/v3-symbols.md),
  [`v4-types.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/v4-types.md).
- [`corpus/cases/`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tree/main/corpus/cases)
  in the repository is a conformance corpus: each case is a
  `template.tramaj` + `ctx.json` + `expected.json` triple (plus optional
  `libs/*.tramaj`) that every implementation must satisfy — treat these as
  runnable, ground-truth examples rather than prose. A case's `meta.json`
  may carry `"profiles"` (`int-float`, `arithmetic`, `int64`, …), naming what an
  implementation must provide before it runs that case. Every implementation
  must provide `int-float` and `arithmetic`; a case that lists another name
  it lacks, such as the integer range it does not have, is skipped.
- See also [`llms.txt`](/raw/llms.txt) for a short machine-readable index of
  the above (Kitchen-Sink serves arbitrary `.txt` files under `/raw/`, so
  this isn't at the conventional `/llms.txt` root path).

## Leaders — what each sigil means

<table>
<tr><th>Leader</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>.</code></td><td>document: <code>.tag(...)</code> element, or <code>.(...)</code> fragment</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$</code></td><td>read a binding or path</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@</code></td><td>define a binding (<code>@name=expr</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>!</code></td><td>emit a constraint, statement position (<code>!constraint(name, ...)</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>symbolic allocation/demand, v3 (<code>?(key)</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>type hole, v4</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--</code></td><td>line comment, to end of line</td></tr>
</table>


## Shape of a program

```
@n=cardinality($ctx.items)
.p("there are `$n` item(s)")
```

An optional block of `@name=expr` bindings, evaluated once each in order,
followed by one root expression. Backtick-quoted `` `$expr` `` interpolates
into a string; a bare `$path` can be a child directly. `a <> b` concatenates
strings, arrays, and objects.

## Documents are ordinary values

There is no separate "template value" type — an element or fragment can be
bound, passed to a function, and returned from one:

```
@kids=.(.p("one"), .p("two"))
@panel=(title, children) => .section(.h2($title), $children)

.main($panel("Deployment", $kids))
```

## Numbers and arithmetic

Two number types, told apart by the text of the literal or of the JSON
number in the context. Nothing converts between them implicitly.

<table>
<tr><th>written</th><th>type</th><th><code>str</code> gives</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>3</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>1_000_000</code></td><td>integer</td><td><code>3</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>1000000</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>3.0</code>, <code>1.5</code>, <code>1e5</code></td><td>float</td><td><code>3.0</code>, <code>1.5</code>, <code>100000.0</code></td></tr>
</table>

- `eq(1, 1.0)` is `false`. `lt`/`lte`/`gt`/`gte` take two integers or two
  floats; a mixed pair is a `TypeMismatch`. Write `gt($x, 0.0)` for a float.
- There are no operators: no `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`. Arithmetic is ten named
  builtins, and there is no subtraction: `sum(a, negate(b))`.

<table>
<tr><th>builtin</th><th>operands</th><th>result</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>sum(…)</code>, <code>product(…)</code></td><td>one or more, all integers or all floats; arrays are flattened</td><td>same type</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>negate(x)</code></td><td>integer or float</td><td>same type</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>quotient(a, b)</code>, <code>inverse(x)</code></td><td>floats only</td><td>float</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>floor-quotient(a, b)</code>, <code>modulo(a, b)</code></td><td>integers only</td><td>integer, rounding toward negative infinity</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>floor(x)</code></td><td>either</td><td>integer</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>round(x)</code></td><td>either</td><td>the nearest integer, a tie going away from zero</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>real(x)</code></td><td>either</td><td>float</td></tr>
</table>

```
@subtotal = sum($ctx.compute, $ctx.storage, negate($ctx.credit))
@share    = branch(0, gt($ctx.total, 0),
                   floor-quotient(product(100, $ctx.used), $ctx.total))
@total    = sum(0.0, map($ctx.lines,
                         (l) => product(real($l.qty), real($l.price))))
```

- A mixed call (`sum(1, 2.5)`, `quotient(1, 2)`) is a `TypeMismatch`. When
  the type of a context number is not known, normalize it: `real($x)` or
  `floor($x)`.
- `sum()` and `sum([])` are a `TypeMismatch`. Seed a fold over a list that
  may be empty: `sum(0, $xs)` or `sum(0.0, $xs)`.
- A zero divisor, an integer overflow and a non-finite float are a
  `NotRepresentable` error, never `null` or `0`. Guard with `branch`, whose
  unselected arm is not evaluated.
- Arithmetic is a profile the host turns on (`tramaj-cli --arithmetic`).
  Without it the ten names are unbound.
- In symbolic mode, a builtin with a symbol among its operands evaluates to
  a term, `{"$term": "sum", "arguments": [...]}`, for the host to evaluate.
  `"$term"` is a reserved object key, like `"$sym"` and `"$type"`.
- The type primitives are `int` and `float` (`@n : int = 3`); there is no
  `number`.

Sorting and number formatting need no profile:

- `sort-by(list, fn)` and `sort-by-descending(list, fn)` order a list by the
  key `fn` gives each element, as in `sort-by($rows, (r) => $r.spend)`. The
  sort is stable, and the keys of one call are all integers, all floats or
  all strings.
- `format-number(x, decimals, group)` writes a number as text:
  `format-number(1234567.891, 2, ",")` is `"1,234,567.89"`.

The reference marks these two and `round` **[§20]**. All six
implementations (PureScript, Haskell, TypeScript, Rust, Go and Python) have
the number split, the ten builtins, the two sorts and `format-number`. They
are on the `main` branch and in no release yet: a released package predates
them, and there every number is a double and these names are unbound.
Templates written before the split:
[Migrating to integers and floats](/migrating-to-integers-and-floats.html).

## Actions are opaque to the language

`action("on-click", "select", {"id": $i.id})` attaches structured data to an
element. The language does not interpret it — a host decides what the key
means. Action keys and adaptation prefixes must be literal strings, never
computed, which is what keeps "what actions can this template emit"
statically answerable.

## Imports

`import(...)` pulls in another program as a library. Parameters can arrive
as an expression, as a `ctx(path)` hole (a static marker that the *importing*
program's own context gets read at that point), or be omitted and supplied
later by calling the import. Imports are lazy — nothing runs until
`.rendered` or `.vals` is read on the result.

## v3 — symbols and constraints (frozen, implemented)

`?(key)` allocates an opaque symbol; only the root program may do this
(libraries raise `AllocationInLibrary`). `!constraint(name, ...)` emits a
constraint. Running in `Symbolic` mode (a host choice, not something the
template declares) wraps the result in a `tramaj/symbolic/1` envelope of
root value, symbols, and constraints instead of a finished document — use
this when the actual values should come from a solver rather than be
supplied up front. Full grammar: [`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html).

## v4 — types (draft, implemented)

`@x : T = e` desugars to `@x=e` plus a `has-type` constraint carrying `T`'s
canonical id. Types are resolved and erased before evaluation — tramaj fixes
type *identity* (canonical ids, string equality), a separate host checker
decides whether values actually satisfy it. Type parameters thread through
imports. Full grammar: [`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html).

## Static analyses available without evaluating

Because imports, action keys, adaptation prefixes, and `ctx(...)` paths are
all literal strings in the syntax, these can all be answered by walking the
AST — no context, no evaluation:

- what a program imports, and with what parameters
- what actions it can possibly emit
- what context paths it reads (`ctx(...)` holes)
- which import parameters are still unsupplied
- which arithmetic builtins it references (`analyze arithmetic`)
- (v3/v4) what constraints and symbols/types it introduces

![Self-check loop for an agent: draft, run analyze, compare the interface with what was asked, revise or hand over](/gen/images/agent-loop.dot.png)

`tramaj-cli`'s `analyze` subcommand exposes these directly — see
[Getting started](/howto-humans.html) for exact invocations. If you're
generating a template and want to
self-check it before handing it to a host, running `analyze` on it is
cheaper and more reliable than re-deriving these properties by re-reading
your own output.

## Common pitfalls

- Don't compute an import name, action key, or `ctx(...)` path from an
  expression — these must be literal strings; the grammar doesn't allow
  otherwise.
- Bindings are visible only *after* their declaration, in order — not
  mutually recursive.
- A library (something loaded via `import`) may not call `?(key)` — only the
  root program may allocate symbols.
- Don't compare or combine an integer with a float: `gt($ratio, 0)` fails
  when `$ratio` is a float. Match the literal to the value, or convert with
  `real`/`floor`.
- Don't write a count as `1.0` or `1e3`: a float renders with its fraction
  (`1.0`, `1000.0`).
- Evaluation order is unconstrained outside of binding precedence: don't
  write templates that rely on side-effect ordering, because there isn't
  one — evaluation is meant to be referentially transparent.

=base:main-css.tramaj-json
{ "format": "css"
, "contents":
  [ ""
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/dev.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/colors.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/article.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/navigation.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/documentation.css\";"
  ]
}

